Mutating-self method dispatched through an interface (TRISC codegen)
======================================================================

Pinned 2026-04-29. The TRISC `TInterfaceBox` codegen for a struct
operand `heap-allocated a copy` of the struct and stored the copy's
address as the interface's `data_ptr`. Inside the dispatched method
`self` pointed at that throwaway heap copy, so any mutation (e.g.
`self.buf = append(self.buf, x)`) silently affected only the copy
and was discarded when the call returned. Direct dispatch (the
language convention for value-type methods) passes `&w` as `self`
and therefore mutates the original — the interface path needs to
match.

Real-world bite: every test in `std/io` that built up a
`ByteWriter` through the `Writer` interface
(`test_writer_interface`, `test_copy`) returned the right `n` from
`w.write(...)` but then asserted `w.to_string() == "hello world"`
on a `w` that was still empty. Both backends (interpreter and
TRISC) had failed previously, but the interpreter was fixed by
wrapping struct receivers in a Cell at dispatch time; TRISC kept
the heap-copy and silently dropped the mutation.

Fix: in `TInterfaceBox` for `StructType`, drop the malloc + copy
and just take the original struct's address. The lifetime risk
(a returned interface of a stack local now dangles) matches
direct dispatch (`*T` of a stack local) and the historical
`PtrType`/`RefType` branch already worked this way.

    module sysl.tests.iface_mutating_self

    interface Sink
        push(v: int) -> int

    struct Bag
        items: []int

    new_bag() -> Bag = Bag((new [0]int)[:0])

    Bag.push(v: int) -> int
        self.items = append(self.items, v)
        return len(self.items)

    Bag.size() -> int = len(self.items)

    feed(s: Sink, vs: []int) -> int
        var total = 0
        for i in 0..<len(vs)
            total = s.push(vs[i])
        return total

    #test
    test_iface_mutating_self_persists() -> unit
        var bag = new_bag()
        val data: [3]int = [10, 20, 30]
        val final_count = feed(bag, data[:])
        assert(final_count == 3, s"final count via iface push: ${final_count}")
        assert(bag.size() == 3, s"after feed, bag.size() should be 3, got ${bag.size()}")
        assert(bag.items[0] == 10, "items[0]")
        assert(bag.items[1] == 20, "items[1]")
        assert(bag.items[2] == 30, "items[2]")
